Fillets: a rounding that rolls on another's contact line against it, the
spines of the two edges running opposite ways (mitcad#343).

The case: both rims of a ring 2.5 thick rounded with 1.5, so that no ball
touches both flat faces: the rounding computed second rolls on the first
one's contact line on the band (0016, RideOnOverlap) and ApplyRides makes
the two blends meet on that line. The line is the first blend's, run its
spine's way; the second blend's side was set on it from its start to its
end, as if the second spine ran the same way. A stripe's spine follows its
edge's curve, and the band's two circles, which start at one point, may
turn opposite ways round the axis (the bottom circle of a ring made from a
cylinder standing upside down): the second blend then used the line
against its own direction, and its face came out reversed (its area
negative) with a wire not closed.

ApplyRides now finds which way the second blend's own line on the support
runs along the first one's (rideRunsAgainst: the tangents at 30 and 70 %
of it, compared with the line's at their projections); where it runs
against it, the second blend's side on the line runs from the line's end
to its start, its ends are checked against the first blend's ends the
other way round, its points there are the first one's the other way round,
and once the stripes are in the data structure the line's transition on
the second blend's surface is turned (turnRidesAgainst), as for the
blends cut by each other between two crossings. On the ring's replayed
body: valid, the file's change of volume within 1.4e-7 of the stored
state's (it failed in both orders of the two rims); on the stored body,
whose circles turn the same way, unchanged.

Upstream: new.
